  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.ontologydesignpatterns.org/ont/dlp/ExtendedDnS.owl#p-sat">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <owl:inverseOf rdf:resource="http://www.ontologydesignpatterns.org/ont/dlp/ExtendedDnS.owl#p-sat-by"/>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="http://www.ontologydesignpatterns.org/ont/dlp/ExtendedDnS.owl#situation"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.ontologydesignpatterns.org/ont/dlp/ExtendedDnS.owl#description"/>
    <rdfs:subPropertyOf rdf:resource="http://www.ontologydesignpatterns.org/ont/dlp/ExtendedDnS.owl#satisfies"/>
    <rdfs:comment rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">P-SAT assumes two satisfaction semantics: redundant satisfaction and qualified satisfaction. In order to allow for a correct implementation of the qualified satisfaction, P-SAT requires that the description exists prior to at least some of the entities in the setting of the satisfying situation. Ontologically, it results that P-SAT also implies a specific dependency of the situation on its description. P-SAT typically applies to plans, projects, designs, methods, techniques, game rules, instructions, punishment rules, constitutive descriptions, sanctions, and strategies.A sample P-SAT qualified satisfaction axiom for plans is given in OWL.</rdfs:comment>
  </rdf:Description>
